package resolver

// Package resolver binds every name in a parsed program to what it
// refers to. It is a port of the Go compiler's package resolver,
// function by function, and binds, reports and recovers exactly as Go's
// does: the same bindings, the same diagnostics, worded the same, at the
// same places, in the same order. The rules, every one of them, are
// documented in full in Go's resolver/doc.go; what follows is the
// resolver's place in the pipeline and where the port differs from Go,
// and why.
//
// It runs after the loader and before the type checker. resolve takes
// an ast.Program, every tree of every package, the package being built
// last, and gives it back with every name bound and the diagnostics it
// found, adding its errors to the program's count; Go's New and Resolve
// are the one function here. Packages are resolved in the program's
// order, every package after those it imports. The builtins it binds
// calls to are a builtin.Set's, and main_optional is Go's MainOptional.
// Without it, a program whose last package is not main is reported,
// "main package not found", and nothing of it is bound;
// missing_main_package tells a caller so beforehand, and the driver
// stops there, where Go's driver checks the unbound program on. Its
// --port-types and --port-bind, harness hooks that tackc's porttest
// compares with Go's checker run in process, check the program on as
// Go's does, so they keep matching it.
//
// What it writes into the trees is Go's: each Ident's def, what the name
// is bound to (see ast.Def), and, for a callee that passed over a
// variable or a parameter of its name, hidden; a conversion's Call.conv;
// a Call's type arguments dropped where Go drops them; a Var's unused,
// unused_deferred and hidden_call; a method's Func.recv_type and its
// type's TypeDecl.methods; and the nodes Go makes, a Var for a union
// pattern's binding, set as its CaseClause's var_node, and a TypeParam
// for a name a case of a switch on a type parameter declares, added to
// the tree of the function they are in.
//
// Names. Go keys its scopes and its tables by names, strings; here each
// name is interned once, as a symbol, an int64 (see Syms), and every
// table is a map keyed by symbols. Go's pointers to declarations, to
// variables and to imports, which its maps are keyed by too, are a
// tree's unit and a node's id here, a Key.
//
// Scopes. Go's scopes are maps linked to the scope enclosing each; a
// function's local scopes open and close as the resolver walks it, so
// here they are a stack, each linked to the one enclosing it, and an
// entry for each name a scope declares or rejects, the latest entry of
// each name a lookup's start (see scope.tck). The global scope is the
// package's members that are no function, and each file's scope its
// imports. Go's pkgResolver for each package, which the packages after
// it read, is maps keyed by the package's index in Res: its members, and
// what its bad nodes may have declared.
//
// Recursion. A tree is as deep as its source makes it: a chain of
// selectors, indexes, calls of methods or binary operators is a node
// deeper for each link, with no nesting the parser counts, and so is the
// target of an assignment, a.b.c[0] = v. Go recurses down each; here
// resolve_expr and resolve_target walk down such a chain in a loop and
// back up it, and the recursion that is left goes only as deep as the
// parser lets a program nest, a level for each call's arguments, each
// bracket, each block and each precedence between. An else if chain is
// a loop in Go too. Go's BadStmts walks a function's body with Walk,
// recursively; here ast.bad_stmts walks it with a stack of its own.
// tackc's porttest runs the deepest shapes at the nesting limit, and
// chains of a hundred thousand links, with the port's stack held to
// 4 MB.
//
// Failures. Go's functions return their diagnostics, which the caller
// appends or drops; here each is appended to Res's list as it is found,
// and settle, which Go hands the diagnostics a binding found, drops
// those reported after a mark instead. Every function returns an error,
// the builtin append's out of memory, which ends the resolution.
// Diagnostics are sorted by file and place when they are rendered, as
// Go's driver sorts them, so their order here matters only between two
// at one place, which is Go's.
